WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.330
To celebrate Cinderella, let’s take a look
at 25 things you probably didn’t know about

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.480
Disney’s live-action remake of their classic
animated movie.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.190
To prepare to play Cinderella, Lily James
practised yoga every day to help get the right

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.590
posture and elegance for her character.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.050
She also took horseback riding lessons for
six weeks.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.130
And to understand how Cinderella maintained
her strength and goodness despite the cruelty

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:30.220
she faced, James researched spirituality,
reading about leaders and pacifists such as

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.220
Gandhi.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:37.519
To help develop the natural rapport real-life
sisters have, director Kenneth Branagh encouraged

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:42.809
actresses Holliday Grainger and Sophie McShera,
who play Cinderella’s wicked stepsisters,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.030
to improvise on set.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:51.710
To make Cinderella’s world feel both believable
and fitting for a fairy tale, production designer

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:57.440
Dante Ferretti mixed historical reality with
fantasy, taking inspiration from Northern

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.979
European architecture of the 16th, 17th, and
18th centuries.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:05.960
Director Kenneth Branagh wanted Cinderella’s
world to have a 19th-century look.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.810
Which meant the movie’s design team could
include earlier styles of architecture into

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.240
their designs.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.200
The set for the palace ballroom took around
9 months to build at Pinewood Studios on the

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.049
007 soundstage.

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:23.079
The ballroom’s design was inspired by French
architecture including the Louvre and the

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:28.290
Hotel de Soubise, both of which have grand
staircases.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:34.580
The ballroom measured 50 yards long, 35 yards
wide and 30 feet high, and had marble floors

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:40.810
and walls, a huge staircase, golden statues,
thousands of flowers, and curtains made from

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.869
more than 2,000 yards of material.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:50.130
In the ballroom, there were 17 huge chandeliers
that were custom-made in Venice, Italy.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:57.229
The chandeliers had almost 5,000 oil candles,
each of which was lit by hand.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:01.549
The movie’s design team built the exterior
of Cinderella’s family’s home on location

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:03.621
at Black Park, a country park in Buckinghamshire,
England.

00:02:03.621 --> 00:02:09.220
The interiors of Cinders’ home, including
the bedrooms, her father’s study, and the

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.600
attic, were built on soundstages at Pinewood
Studios.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:19.330
Although the story takes place around the
1830s, costume designer Sandy Powell used

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.700
artistic license to do what was best for each
character.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:31.040
So, the costumes aren’t strictly 19th century,
but rather a 1940s version of that era.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.300
Powell approached the film like a storybook
for children.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.350
In other words, she made her designs bright
and colourful with fairly-easy references

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.230
as to who was good and who was evil.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.330
Costume designer Sandy Powell didn’t want
Cinderella’s usual outfit to be rags or

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.810
a patchwork dress.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.560
Instead, Cinders wears a dress that gradually
deteriorates and fades, but looks like something

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.610
she would have worn in happier days when her
father was still alive.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:00.870
The dress is made of aqua cotton voile and
is influenced by a 1920s floral print with

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.880
big pale pink flowers almost hidden in the
material.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.600
Cinderella’s ball gown took months to make
with numerous prototypes, fittings and trials,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.630
which included moving and dancing, along the
way.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:18.870
To give the dress a light, almost weightless,
look, costume designer Sandy Powell used many

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:25.080
layers of very fine fabric in different shades
of blue, which gave the gown its watery lilac

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.080
blue colour.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.010
Nine different versions of Cinderella’s
ball gown were made, featuring more than 270

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:37.100
yards of fabric, lots of petticoats, more
than 10,000 Swarovski crystals, and over 3

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.520
miles of hems.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:46.470
It took 16 people 550 hours to make Cinderella’s
silk organza wedding dress.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:51.230
Unlike Cinders’ ball gown, only one wedding
dress was made.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:57.450
For Cate Blanchett’s wicked stepmother costumes,
designer Sandy Powell took inspiration from

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:02.370
1940s photos of movie stars such as Marlene
Dietrich and Joan Crawford.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.380
Powell used a strong jewel-tone colour palette
and a lot of black for the stepmother’s

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.180
clothes to give her a sharp edge, in spite
of her beauty.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:16.260
And she gave the stepmother and her daughters
so many incredible outfits to emphasis the

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:21.380
fact that they were spending all the money
left by Cinderella’s father on their own

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.910
clothes.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.350
The stepsisters’ outfits were deliberately
designed with the cheapest fabrics to look

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.670
over the top and tasteless.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:34.180
As well as making the sisters look silly,
this also helped keep the focus on the stepmother.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:40.790
Of all the characters in the live-action Cinderella,
costume designer Sandy Powell says the Prince

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:45.560
is the one who looks most like his character
in Disney’s animated Cinderella movie.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:50.410
In this new film, the Prince’s costumes
often feature shades of blue to accentuate

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.850
actor Richard Madden’s blue eyes.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.910
And even though it would have been more historically
accurate for the Prince’s uniform to have

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:03.610
tight white breeches with baggy knees, Powell
thought fitted trousers looked more flattering.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:10.430
Helena Bonham Carter’s Fairy Godmother gown
was almost 4 feet wide, made up of over 130

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:17.100
yards of fabric, featured 10,000 Swarovski
crystals, and had 400 LED lights sewn into

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.970
the material so it would light up when she
cast a spell.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:25.560
There were more than 200 extras in Cinderella’s
ballroom scenes.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:34.190
These included 25 guards, 20 servants, 54
professional dancers and 30 orchestra members.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:38.740
The costumes worn by guests at the royal Ball
were inspired by ballroom dance scenes in

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.960
classic films such as Italian director Luchino
Visconti’s The Leopard and American director

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.080
Alexander Hall’s Once Upon A Time.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:53.050
Over 3 months of planning and preparation,
including casting, wardrobe fittings and rehearsals,

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.060
were needed for the whole ballroom sequence.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.290
And more than 35 assistant directors helped
oversee it.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:05.120
The shape of Cinderella’s glass slipper
is based on a shoe from the 1890s which costume

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.530
designer Sandy Powell found in a shoe museum
in Northampton, England.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:15.270
Powell looked at different ways of making
a glass shoe, but because the shoe needed

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:18.710
to sparkle, she realised it had to be made
of crystal.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:22.930
So, over many months, she worked with the
Austrian crystal company Swarovski and they

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.940
developed a special piece of machinery to
make the shoe.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.410
Eight copies of the slipper were made, however
none of them were actually worn in the film.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:35.990
Instead, they were used as props, for example
in the scenes where maidens try on the slipper,

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:36.990
and where the slipper is smashed.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:42.894
During the ballroom scenes, the filmmakers
used five cameras to make sure they caught

00:06:42.894 --> 00:06:43.894
every moment in the dance sequences from as
many positions as possible.

00:06:43.894 --> 00:06:47.500
And they also used two cranes to get a sweeping
effect and show the scale of the set and the

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.210
dance routines.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:55.920
To make Cinderella appear as natural as possible,
the movie’s make-up designer kept Lily James’

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.520
make-up minimal, and used blusher to highlight
her emotions.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:05.100
So, in scenes where Cinders is sad, they reduced
the blusher, making her look a little pale.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.420
While in scenes where she was excited or falling
in love, they used more blusher.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:14.590
To give Cinders more sparkle in the ballroom
scene, the make-up team applied a light-reflecting

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:20.570
lotion mixed with white glitter to Lily James’s
skin, and glitter to her eyelids.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:23.660
And crystals were placed in her hair.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:29.139
It took 5 hours to get each extra ready for
the ballroom scenes, as each one needed to

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:32.000
be dressed, made up, photographed, and charted.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.570
The design of Cinderella’s iconic carriage
was inspired by jewellery and jewellery cases.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:44.380
And because the pumpkin’s transformation
takes place in the greenhouse in Cinderella’s

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:48.840
garden, the design team incorporated elements
from the greenhouse into the carriage’s

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:49.840
design.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.740
For example, the seat Cinderella sits on is
from the greenhouse.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:57.460
The carriage was 10 feet high, 17 feet long,
and weighed nearly 2 tons.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:02.490
In fact, it was too heavy to be pulled by
real horses, so the design team had to remove

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:06.530
some of the ironwork used in it and replace
it with light-weight sculptures.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:10.310
Well there you have it, 25 things you probably
didn't know about Cinderella!

